WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.040
Good morning, Hank. It's Tuesday.

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:04.760
So on the day I met Aida and Majeeda at the Azraq Refugee Camp in Northern Jordan,

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:06.940
I also met sixteen-year-old Hussam.

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:07.440
"Hi."

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:08.160
"John, how are you?"

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:08.980
"Good, nice to meet you."

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:09.800
"Yeah, nice to meet you too."

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:13.620
Hussam is a truly extraordinary kid who lives with his mother and younger brother

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.760
in a shelter with very little furniture, except a bookshelf,

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.040
which contains the Quran and a couple other books.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.820
I began by asking Hussam how he learned to speak English.

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:23.660
"Actually, I learned here in the camp."

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:24.720
"You learned in the camp?"

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.220
"Yeah, three months of hard study."

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:27.340
"Three months?"

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:32.220
"I download some videos from YouTube and I learnt speaking, listening."

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.360
That's right, he learned from YouTube.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:37.440
Well, and he works with a tutor, who's also a Syrian refugee.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.120
"I go to him everyday, everyday. Nightly, in the morning, when I have free time,

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.780
I just go up to him and say, 'Hey, teach me English.'

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:47.240
He has two accents, American and Britain."

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:48.040
[Laughter]

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:51.580
Lizziana and I are laughing because for both of us, that's one more accent than we have.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:56.180
Hussam also studied English a little bit back in Syria. In fact, he was in English class when his school

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:56.980
was bombed.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:59.820
"Suddenly, we heard the English teacher shouting, 'Lay down!'

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:02.500
And all the students were laying on the ground.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:06.260
So, just- An airplane shelled the school with missiles.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:08.820
And many students, my friends, died and were injured.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:12.180
The girls were crying, and I had my friend who died.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:13.380
"Sa'ad Sultan Alghanim"

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:18.240
His name, and actually, it was terrible. Yeah. It was terrible."

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:19.960
"So..."

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:23.460
"And I can see that you think about your friend often."

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:26.720
"Yeah, actually, I will show you his photos and the school."

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:28.240
"Yeah, can you show me?"

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:32.760
This happened to me often in Jordan. The refugees I met almost never had printed photographs.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:37.820
Instead, their pictures of friends and family were on phones; sometimes on nonfunctioning phones

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:40.300
they kept charged only to access the pictures.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:43.620
Hussam first showed me a picture of blood in the floor of his classroom.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:47.840
And then he showed me his only picture of his friend. It was of a teen boy's corpse,

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:49.860
his dead eyes staring up at the camera.

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.360
I asked him if this is why his family had left Syria.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:57.520
"Yeah, this moment. They said, 'No school. No working. No life.'

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.720
Syria wasn't safe, so we should go to Jordan to receive a better life."

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.000
But since then, Hussam's family has spread out.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.980
"I have three parts of my family. One in Syria, one in Germany, and we are here,

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:11.040
and actually I have my brother in Turkey."

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:15.480
His brother in Turkey is on scholarship at university. He has two married sisters still in Syria,

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:20.400
and his father, an engineer, and older brother took the boats to cross into Europe more than a year ago.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:25.060
Hussam, his mother, and younger brother, are hoping to be allowed to reunite with them soon in Germany.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:29.960
Hussam seems to genuinely love learning. He's a very successful tenth grader at the refugee camp's

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.020
high school. He also loves writing stories.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:38.720
"I like to write adventure stories. So, somebody who's lost in the forest,

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:44.320
and he's found something in the night, and things like that I can write. So I like it."

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:48.640
But when I asked him if he wanted to be a writer or a translator when he grew up, he said no.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:52.140
"I hope to be into engineering, like my father, who's in Germany.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:59.700
To finish my studies. When I finish, I go to a company in Syria, which helps Syrian- People in Syria."

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:01.500
"And to help rebuild it, that would be your dream?"

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:06.120
"Yeah, rebuild it. This is my dream, actually. Syria, it's my country. It's the green country. I love it so much."

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.240
This young man has not seen his father, his sisters, or two of his brothers in more than a year,

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:16.000
and he knows that they can only be reunited in a safe, peaceful Syria. And that is his dream.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.280
To rebuild that country.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:22.060
Hussam also loves to sing. In fact, he sang to us about Syria while his mother wept.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:28.040
[Singing in Arabic]

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:32.560
And so this young singer and writer will hopefully one day soon be a engineer.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:37.820
And I really admire that professional goal. Hussam is a brilliant and tremendously disciplined kid.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:40.980
I mean, he learned English in three months. He could do anything.

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:45.740
But his greatest ambition is to bring his talents to the kind of work his country will need the most.

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:50.620
And to me, that' true heroism. Godspeed Hussam. May your dreams come true.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:55.340
May you be a soldier in the army of engineers that rebuilds our country.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:57.260
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:10.620
[Singing in Arabic]

